I came to Stanford to explore  transformation it is what matters most  to me and is a word that has caused me  as much pain as it is pride I'm not  alone society is as obsessed with  transformation as I am  we're fascinated by the before and after  who you were then and who you are now  on Instagram it's the ten year challenge  in nature it's the caterpillar turns  butterfly I've spent my career in  education facilitating change as a  mixed-race Jewish woman I wanted to  ensure that organizations changed to  embrace marginalized voices so I worked  for organizations that created  transformative experiences while they  were undergoing drastic internal change  things like scaling local to national  transitioning CEOs infusing equity and  inclusion I had a front-row seat to  transformation of all kinds it showed me  that even the experts still struggle to  create and define transformation and  that's the thing do any of us really  know what transformation means when we  say it because if we want to change  lives organizations and the world we  need to start defining how we do it I'm  here to end the before and after  dichotomy I want to shift us from seeing  transformation as an outcome to a  process and there's no process more  famous no transformation metaphor more  cliched then the caterpillar turns  butterfly  I actually love this metaphor and as a  former elementary school science teacher  I'm very familiar with it the  caterpillar to butterfly captures  transformations magic and mystery yet  what happens inside the chrysalis face  is unquestioned unheard-of and untouched  and that's the mistake we should be  talking about the chrysalis the true  hero the chrysalis holds the key to how  we can define transformation so let's  focus on the chrysalis because what  happens inside is absolutely  mind-blowing a caterpillar makes her  chrysalis from the inside out to prepare  she anchors herself to a home usually a  twig  she then bursts her head open to shed  her skin the underlying layer is the  chrysalis the chrysalis protects her and  camouflages with the outside worlds with  the hope that no one notices what's  about to happen inside the chrysalis she  then actively breaks down  she liquefies turning completely into  mush the only thing left are her  essential structures for breathing yet  even as mush she knows how to rebuild  she builds each structure carefully her  legs her wings the patterns on her body  and when ready she breaks free until  finally she can fly  when caterpillars break down they know  how to create new better versions of  themselves they can even remember their  time as a caterpillar once they became a  butterfly what they smelled what they  liked what scared them at each step this  transformation is active messy  intentional and personal this is how we  can define transformation this is the  magic and I've learned this firsthand  I experienced a couple of personal  transformations it took me years to  truly accept my identity as a black and  white Jewish woman yet what truly taught  me the process of transformation to  define it in real time was of all things  a bad breakup a couple years ago I was  ending a five-year relationship starting  a new job and learning to live in a new  city feelings of finding myself were  front and center I was angry I was sad  and I was surrounded by people that  created transformation every day how do  you do that I never got that question  answered which is why I'm here  waxing nature metaphors and divulging my  story of liquefying  we need to talk about the breakdown I  went through the same steps as that  caterpillar step 1 I incurred the San  Francisco Bay Area and later Stanford  transformation often happens in these  immersive learning experiences because  you're rooted in a place to do the work  step 2 shed skin and form protection I  simultaneously switched jobs moved ended  their relationship and applied to  business school in many ways my  chrysalis has been my business school  experience from application to today  step 3  breakdown I definitely broke I sat alone  and slept alone I cried every single day  I screamed I don't scream I laid on my  bedroom floor my version of mush I was  so lost step 4 thoughtfully put yourself  back together  I found B I started writing again after  five years poetry mostly and letters I  write in my wrongs and accepted there  were wrongs that I would never be able  to write I practiced presence balance  and gratitude I actively intentionally  changed for the better  step 5  break free fly and remember where you  came from to be honest I'm not here yet  I'm in the messy mushy middle and I love  being in my chrysalis  it gives me direction for one to say yes  and no and for how to prepare for what's  next that's the process and we can all  prepare for what's next because it's so  much more than my transformation its  life so many of us are at Stanford to  search for transformation to welcome it  to facilitate it yet we don't just come  to Stanford to be transformed we do the  work we thoughtfully choose  opportunities that constitute our legs  and wings our careers and organizations  maybe we're all in the messy middle at  Stanford and as a society right now we  have the power and process to be better  because we can do more than change lives  organizations in the world we can  transform them but it won't be enough to  repeat the words we're gonna have to  break them and ourselves all the way  down in order to build back up into  something beautiful  [Applause]  [Music]
 

